1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,280
From here he would go on to live a fairly isolated life traveling around Europe moving to and from different climates most suitable for his poor health and living off his small university pension.
叙述者说，此后他开始过较孤独的生活，在欧洲辗转，寻找适合健康状况的气候，靠少量大学退休金生活。

2
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:16,869
He would live primarily and most notably in the swiss alps where he would spend the majority of his remaining sane life.
他主要住在瑞士阿尔卑斯山区，并在那里度过了后来仍保持神志清醒的大部分岁月。

3
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:25,159
Throughout this time in between spells of being bedridden by his ailments a devastating failed love ordeal degrading friendships and family relations.
这段时间里，他时而因疾病卧床，经历感情失意，友情和家人关系也逐渐恶化，

4
00:00:25,159 --> 00:00:31,200
And depressive and nihilistic states n would spend most of his time walking thinking and writing.
也陷入抑郁和虚无的状态；叙述者说，他主要把时间用在散步、思考和写作上。

5
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:36,239
Finding solace meaning and reason to continue through his pursuit of philosophy.
他在哲学的探索中寻找安慰、意义，以及继续生活的理由。

6
00:00:36,239 --> 00:00:48,120
During this time he would produce his most influential works including human all to human the gay science thus spoke zarathustra beyond good and evil and on the genealogy of morals.
这段时期他写下重要著作，包括视频所提到的《人性的，太人性的》《快乐的科学》《查拉图斯特拉如是说》《善恶的彼岸》《道德谱系》。

7
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:53,959
In these works n would lay both the groundwork and early constructions of a new sort of philosophy.
在这些著作中，叙述者认为他为一种新的哲学打下了基础，开始初步建构。

8
00:00:53,959 --> 00:01:00,309
A philosophy that would essentially loosen the bolts of all contemporary certainties all notions of good and evil.
一种仿佛要拧松当时所有确定观念螺栓的哲学，包括善与恶的概念，

9
00:01:00,319 --> 00:01:08,480
All knowledge of true and false right and wrong god is dead god remains dead and we have killed him.
以及真假、对错的知识。随后视频引述：“上帝死了，上帝仍然死着，而是我们杀死了他。”

10
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:12,239
How shall we comfort ourselves the murderers of all murderers.
“我们这些凶手中的凶手，要怎样安慰自己？”

11
00:01:12,239 --> 00:01:17,959
What was holiest and mightiest of all that the world has yet owned has bled to death under our knives.
“这个世界曾拥有的最神圣、最强大的存在，在我们的刀下流血至死。”

12
00:01:17,959 --> 00:01:20,880
Is not the greatness of this deed too great for us.
“这一行为的伟大，难道不是超出了我们吗？”

13
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:25,600
Must we ourselves not become gods simply to appear worthy of it.
“难道我们自己不该成为神，才能显得配得上它吗？”

14
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:29,039
This is perhaps one of n's most quoted and important passages.
这也许是叙述者所说的，尼采最常被引用、也最重要的段落之一。

15
00:01:29,039 --> 00:01:39,080
It is in this line line god is dead that we find not n's celebration of humanity's lost faith but his stark intense concern of warning for what it meant.
叙述者说，在“上帝死了”这句话中，看到的不是庆祝信仰消失，而是对其含义的强烈担忧与警示。

16
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:44,360
The collapse of christian faith brought with it in n's mind the collapse of everything built on it.
在他的解读中，基督教信仰的崩塌，也意味着建立在其上的一切崩塌，

17
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:48,960
The whole of european morality its rationals and its values.
包括欧洲的整套道德、其理据和价值观；rationals 是自动字幕用词，待原声核对。

18
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:58,029
He both predicted and feared that with this collective revelation without sufficient replacement humanity would be left to struggle with no clear system or meaning.
他预见并担忧：若这种共同认识没有合适的替代，人类将面临缺乏明确体系与意义的挣扎，

19
00:01:58,039 --> 00:02:02,199
And devolve into widespread disp in the form of nihilism.
并陷入某种普遍的绝望，以虚无主义的形式出现；原字幕 disp 截断，待听辨。

20
00:02:02,199 --> 00:02:11,199
One of n's key ideas at the foundation of his attempt to resolve this issue is the recognition that there is in fact no universal objective truth to be known.
叙述者说，他为解决这一问题提出的基础想法之一，是没有可被认识的普遍客观真理。

21
00:02:11,199 --> 00:02:15,000
There are no facts only interpretations he wrote.
视频接着引述：“没有事实，只有解释。”

22
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,120
N denied the very construct of any sort of capital t truth and suggested that all attempts to find one were woefully misguided.
叙述者说，他否认某种绝对“真理”的构想，并认为追求这种真理会严重误入歧途，

23
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:27,400
And actually the source of disconnect preventing modern man from rediscovering any meaning in life.
甚至是造成人与意义脱节、阻碍现代人重新发现生活意义的根源。

24
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:35,080
The pursuit of universal objectivity or meaning beyond this life took the spirit out of the present earthly human experience of meaning.
追求超越今生的普遍客观性或意义，会抽走当下、尘世中人类体验意义的精神，

25
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:38,919
Which is inherently subjective independent and expressive.
而叙述者认为，这种意义体验本来就是主观、独立且富有表达性的。

26
00:02:38,919 --> 00:02:43,280
Because of this n would direct his attention primarily to the arts and humanities.
因此，他把注意力主要转向艺术与人文学科。

27
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:50,000
Believing that creative acts and experiences be it things like music philosophy literature theater and so on.
叙述者说，他相信创造性行为与体验，例如音乐、哲学、文学、戏剧等，

28
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,240
Could be used as essential means to communicate deeper truths and fill the void of higher connection and meaning.
可以成为传达更深层真理、填补更高联结与意义空缺的重要途径。

29
00:02:56,240 --> 00:03:05,600
Although as n explored this theory he would find that cultural arts and humanities were susceptible to becoming dried out academic and or commodified.
不过，他探索这个理论时，又发现文化、艺术与人文可能变得枯燥、学院化，或者商品化，

30
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:08,520
Often losing their luster and dependability.
从而失去原有光彩，也不再可靠。

31
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:17,520
From here he would turn his attention towards creating a philosophy that detached the individual from dependence on any collective experience or cultural mechanisms.
于是，他转向建立一种哲学，让个体不再依赖某种集体经验或文化机制，

32
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:22,870
And rather focused on the individual pursuit of creative expression and subjective greatness.
而把重点放在个人对创造性表达、主观卓越的追求上，

33
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:27,800
Placing the creation of meaning squarely in the hands of each individual.
把创造意义的责任，明确放到每一个个体手中。
